metric constraints create a more complex strategic landscape where the Sprague-Grundy values deviate from the simple Nim-sum formula.\n\n## Computational Exploration and Conjectures\n\nThe research extends beyond the proven case of k = 4m + 3 through computational investigation. For k = 4m + 1 (such as k = 5), computational evidence suggests:\n\nConjecture: P-positions satisfy (x-1) ⊕ y ⊕ (z-1) = 0\n\nThis indicates a \"shifted\" Nim-sum formula, where the effective coordinates for the XOR calculation are modified. However, for even values of k, the computational results show irregular patterns with no apparent simple formula, suggesting fundamentally different mathematical behavior.\n\nThe computational approach uses both Mathematica and the Combinatorial Game Suite (CGSuite) to verify theoretical results and explore parameter ranges beyond analytical tractability. This hybrid methodology demonstrates the power of combining rigorous mathematical proof with computational exploration in modern game theory research.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProof of a Conjecture on the Growth of the Maximal Resistance Distance in a Linear 3--Tree (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392134378_Proof_of_a_Conjecture_on_the_Growth_of_the_Maximal_Resistance_Distance_in_a_Linear_3--Tree)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Proof of a Conjecture on the Growth of the Maximal Resistance Distance in a Linear 3--Tree ... Barret, Evans, and Francis conjectured that if G is the straight linear 3-tree with n vertices and H is the straight linear 3-tree with n+1 vertices then $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty} r_{H} (1, n+1) - r_G(1,n) = \\frac{1}{14},$ where $r_G(u,v)$ and $r_H(u,v)$ are the resistance distance between vertices u and v in graphs G and H respectively.\n\nPreprint\n\n# Proof of a Conjecture on the Growth of the Maximal Resistance Distance in a Linear 3--Tree\n\n  * May 2025\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2505.20539\n\nAuthors:\n\nEmily J. Evans\n\nEmily J. Evans\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nRussell Jay Hendel\n\nRussell Jay Hendel\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nBarret, Evans, and Francis conjectured that if G is the straight linear 3-tree with n vertices and H is the straight linear 3-tree with n+1 vertices then $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty} r_{H} (1, n+1) - r_G(1,n) = \\frac{1}{14},$ where $r_G(u,v)$ and $r_H(u,v)$ are the resistance distance between vertices u and v in graphs G and H respectively. In this paper, we prove the conjecture by looking at the determinants of deleted Laplacian matrices. The proof uses a Laplace expansion method on a family of determinants to determine the underlying recursion this family satisfies and then uses routine linear algebra methods to obtain an exact Binet formula for the n-th term.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (25)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nCircuit‐theory applications to connectivity science and conservation\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Nov 2018\n  * CONSERV BIOL\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/database?tag=resistance-distance)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * ## Asymptotic resistance increment conjecture for straight linear K-trees ... open graph-theory linear-trees resistance-distance\n\n# 10 problems\n\n[Button: Trending][Button: Newest][Button: Featured][Button: Likes]\n\nFilters 1\n\n[Button: Post Problem]\n\nMatching Tag: resistance-distance\n\n  * ## Resistance-distance bound for connected balanced digraphs\n\nAbout 24 years old\n\nResistance-di